specs/hal-engine-awaitable-session-store/spec.md:27:- **GDPR.** A store call that rejects while releasing a session is caught and logged with the session id, so a cleanup that failed is reported rather than silently recorded as done ([validated by: logs a rejecting evict rather than dropping it](../../src/transport/ws/connectionHandler.test.ts#L192)).
specs/hal-engine-awaitable-session-store/spec.md:31:- A synchronous implementation needs no change: `T` is assignable to `T | Promise<T>` in a return position, so `InMemorySessionStore` satisfies the widened interface unedited and its own suite passes with no `await` added ([validated by: accepts a fully synchronous store and a fully asynchronous one](../../src/infrastructure/stores/sessionStoreAwaitable.test.ts#L18)).
specs/hal-engine-awaitable-session-store/spec.md:32:- The one observable change is timing rather than type: the `connected` frame now arrives a tick later than it did. A client that waits for the frame, as the protocol already requires, cannot tell the difference ([validated by: delivers a frame sent before the connected frame arrives](../../src/transport/awaitedSessionCreate.test.ts#L33)).
specs/hal-engine-before-tool-call-hook/spec.md:12:- `beforeToolCall?: (session: ChatSession, call: ToolCall) => Promise<ToolResponse | undefined>` is an optional member of `OrchestratorHooks`, and `ToolCall` and `ToolResponse` are importable from the package root beside it, as `example/full-config.ts` does under `npm run typecheck:example` ([validated by: hands the executor the very object the model produced when the policy returns undefined](../../src/transport/ws/beforeToolCall.test.ts#L109)).
---
8:`OrchestratorHooks` is the engine's only lifecycle seam, and `HalEngineConfig.orchestrator.hooks` is the supported way to install it: `createHalEngine` forwards the set to `createChatOrchestrator`, so a hook a deployer passes through the factory reaches the running orchestrator rather than sitting unread on the config object. This spec pins that forwarding and the measured semantics the seam already carries, so the hook-based features that follow in this epic — transparency disclosure, audit and usage recording, human oversight, provider failover — specify against real behaviour rather than against what the hook names suggest.
25:These properties of `src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.ts` record the measured semantics that hook features specify against. The `onError` boundary remains out of scope; issue #93 defines the aggregate usage delivered after tool rounds.
28:- `afterModelResponse` is called once per user message after the round loop exits and receives the summed provider-call usage for the entire turn, so tool rounds are included in a deployer's billing or audit total ([validated by: reports the summed usage from every provider call in a turn with two tool rounds](../../src/orchestration/chatOrchestratorUsage.acceptance.test.ts#L44)).
29:- A provider round that reports no usage adds nothing to the turn's total, so the rounds that did report are still counted, and a turn in which no round reported passes `undefined` to `afterModelResponse` ([validated by: counts reported rounds while leaving a turn with no reported usage undefined](../../src/orchestration/chatOrchestratorUsage.acceptance.test.ts#L67)). The same contract for a single summation step is pinned at the helper ([validated by: sums each token field across reported rounds and stays undefined when no round reported](../../src/orchestration/orchestratorHelpers.test.ts#L29)).

